+<h3 id="description">Description</h3>
+<p>
+<code>xmloxide</code>'s DTD parser recursed without a depth bound when parsing the
+parenthesised content model of an <code>&lt;!ELEMENT&gt;</code> declaration. A small document
+whose internal DTD subset contains many nested <code>(</code> drives unbounded mutual
+recursion between <code>DtdParser::parse_content_particle</code> and
+<code>DtdParser::parse_content_spec_group</code>, exhausting the stack and aborting the
+process.</p>
+<p>The internal DTD subset is parsed during ordinary document parsing, so this is
+reachable through the default parse entry points (for example
+<code>Document::parse_str</code>) on any input that carries a <code>DOCTYPE</code>. The existing
+<code>ParseOptions::max_depth</code> limit does not prevent it: it bounds element nesting,
+not DTD content-model nesting. A stack overflow cannot be caught as an error,
+so any process that parses attacker-controlled XML with <code>xmloxide</code> can be
+crashed with a small payload.</p>
+<p>Fixed in 0.4.4: a parenthesis-nesting depth counter is threaded through the
+content-model parser, and nesting deeper than 256 levels is rejected with a
+normal <code>ParseError</code>. Well-formed DTDs are unaffected.</p>
+<p>Applications that cannot upgrade should reject or strip the <code>DOCTYPE</code> from
+untrusted input before parsing.</p>
